# Copy to .env and fill in. .env holds your database password and is
# gitignored; don't commit it.
#
# Copy it before you run ./scripts/setup_postgres.sh. With no .env in place
# that script writes one of its own, and it holds five lines and nothing else:
# DATABASE_URL, TEST_DATABASE_URL, PRISM_GENAI_CLIENT_LOCATION=us-central1, a
# placeholder PRISM_GENAI_CLIENT_PROJECT and an empty PRISM_GDA_PROJECTS. None
# of the rest of the Google Cloud section below is written, and an empty
# PRISM_GDA_PROJECTS leaves the app with no agents to offer. Over an existing
# .env the script rewrites DATABASE_URL and TEST_DATABASE_URL and touches
# nothing else. So copy this file, fill in Google Cloud, then run the script.

# --- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Set DATABASE_URL (any PostgreSQL) or INSTANCE_CONNECTION_NAME (Cloud SQL, via
# the connector). INSTANCE_CONNECTION_NAME wins if both are set: the connector
# dials the instance itself and DATABASE_URL is then ignored. If neither is
# set, Prism falls back to postgresql://localhost/prism.
#
# ./scripts/setup_postgres.sh rewrites this line and TEST_DATABASE_URL below, so
# the "prism" role here is only a placeholder. That script creates a role named
# after DB_USER, which it defaults to your login name, not "prism". If you are
# not running the script, put your own role and password in instead: the role
# "prism" does not exist unless you created it yourself.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://prism:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/prism
# INSTANCE_CONNECTION_NAME=my-project:us-central1:my-instance
# DB_USER=postgres
# DB_PASS=
# DB_NAME=prism
# DB_IP_TYPE=PUBLIC

# DB_PORT is the host-side port ./scripts/setup_postgres.sh publishes the
# container on, and DB_HOST is the host it dials. Neither belongs here: that
# script never reads .env, so both have to be in the shell environment.
#   DB_PORT=5433 ./scripts/setup_postgres.sh
# It writes the URLs here to match. A port mapping is fixed when a container is
# created, so pointing DB_PORT somewhere else makes the script remove and
# recreate postgres_local. The databases sit in the postgres_local_data volume
# and survive that. A container created before that volume existed does not
# carry them across, and the script says so and asks first before it removes
# one.

# Where the tests run. ./scripts/run_tests.sh requires this and points
# DATABASE_URL at it, so a test run can't reach the database above.
# ./scripts/run_e2e.sh derives its own <name>_e2e database from it. Same
# placeholder role as DATABASE_URL above, and the script rewrites this line too.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://prism:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/prism_test

# Set this to name the browser tier's database yourself instead. The name has
# to end in "e2e", optionally after a _ or a -. tests/db_guard.py only clears a
# trailing marker, so prism_e2e works and prism_e2e_db does not.
# E2E_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://prism:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/prism_e2e

# --- Server -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the development server binds. src/prism/ui/app.py reads both, and
# ./scripts/run_app.sh prints them.
# PRISM_HOST=127.0.0.1
# PORT=8080

# ./scripts/run_prod.sh reads PORT too, and binds gunicorn to 0.0.0.0:$PORT.
# TIMEOUT is gunicorn's --timeout in seconds, and that script is its only
# reader. A trial can outlive a normal request timeout, hence the large
# default.
# TIMEOUT=3600

# --- Google Cloud -----------------------------------------------------------
# Prism uses Application Default Credentials. Before the first run:
#   gcloud auth application-default login
#
# Path to a credentials file, read by the Google client libraries. Leave it
# unset locally: the login above is the normal path, and on Cloud Run the
# runtime service account is picked up with no file at all. Set it only where
# neither is available, such as inside the container, where there is no gcloud
# and the README mounts your ADC file in.
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/adc.json
#
# Comma-separated projects whose Conversational Analytics (Gemini Data
# Analytics) agents Prism should offer. Leave this empty and the project
# dropdown on /agents/onboard/new and /agents/onboard/existing renders with no
# options and no error, so there is no way to add an agent.
PRISM_GDA_PROJECTS=my-project

# Comma-separated regions to look for those agents in. Discovery builds one
# client per region, so trim this to the ones you use.
# PRISM_GDA_LOCATIONS=global,us,eu

# Project and region for the Gen AI calls behind the LLM judge.
PRISM_GENAI_CLIENT_PROJECT=my-project
PRISM_GENAI_CLIENT_LOCATION=us-central1

# --- BigQuery evaluation exporter (optional) --------------------------------
# Off by default. When on, finished runs are mirrored into BigQuery. The
# dataset is created if it doesn't exist.
# BIGQUERY_EXPORT_ENABLED=false
# BIGQUERY_EXPORT_PROJECT=my-project
# BIGQUERY_EXPORT_DATASET=prism_evals
# BIGQUERY_EXPORT_LOCATION=US

# --- Development ------------------------------------------------------------
# Turns on the Werkzeug debugger and reloader. Don't set this on a deployed
# instance: the debugger runs arbitrary code for anyone who can reach the port.
# PRISM_DEBUG=false

# How the agent clients talk to the API: live, record, or replay. Leave it
# unset. The browser tier sets it to replay for itself, and ./scripts/run_e2e.sh
# takes --record and --live, so a value here applies to every run you do from
# then on.
# PRISM_AGENT_BACKEND=live

# Where cassettes are read from and written to. The browser tier overrides this
# per spec from tests/e2e/conftest.py, so a value here only reaches
# ./scripts/run_tests.sh and a bare pytest.
# PRISM_CASSETTE_DIR=tests/cassettes
